\section{Configuration and TeX-safe primitives}

The generated LaTeX is intentionally plain.  The QR code should be easy to place
without forcing the surrounding document to adopt a special theme.

<<constants>>=
LATEX_ESCAPES = {
    "\\": r"\textbackslash{}",
    "&": r"\&",
    "%": r"\%",
    "$": r"\$",
    "#": r"\#",
    "_": r"\_",
    "{": r"\{",
    "}": r"\}",
    "~": r"\textasciitilde{}",
    "^": r"\textasciicircum{}",
    '"': r'\string"',
}

DEFAULT_LAYOUT = "slide"
VALID_LAYOUTS = {"slide", "article", "article+qr", "auto"}

SLIDE_TEXT_WIDTH = "0.62\\textwidth"
SLIDE_QR_WIDTH = "0.33\\textwidth"
ARTICLE_TEXT_WIDTH = "0.62\\textwidth"
ARTICLE_QR_WIDTH = "0.32\\textwidth"
@

The escape helper is small, but it is what keeps ordinary poll questions from
breaking TeX when they happen to contain an underscore, percent sign, or other
special character.  The double quote is not special in plain LaTeX, but
babel makes it an active shorthand in Swedish and German, where [["a]]
sets an umlaut, so a quoted word in a prompt would lose its quotes;
[[\string"]] prints the character itself under any language.
[[\textquotedbl]] would be the obvious spelling, but it fails outright
under the default OT1 encoding.

<<functions>>=
def _escape_latex(text: str) -> str:
    return "".join(LATEX_ESCAPES.get(char, char) for char in text)
@

URL construction and configuration lookup are deliberately separate from
rendering.  The renderer should only see resolved values; otherwise every
layout branch would need to duplicate the same precedence rules for explicit
arguments, environment variables, and stored configuration.

<<functions>>=
def _join_url(base_url: str, prefix: str) -> str:
    return f"{base_url.rstrip('/')}/{prefix}"


def _resolve_environment(explicit: str | None) -> str | None:
    if explicit is not None:
        return explicit or None

    env_override = os.environ.get("MENTIPY_QUESTION_ENVIRONMENT")
    if env_override:
        return env_override

    return get_setting(
        QUESTION_ENVIRONMENT_CONFIG,
        DEFAULT_QUESTION_ENVIRONMENT,
    )


def _resolve_show_url(explicit: bool | None) -> bool:
    if explicit is not None:
        return explicit

    return bool(get_setting(SHOW_URL_CONFIG, DEFAULT_SHOW_URL))
@

\section{Translatable labels}
\label{sec:slide-labels}

The body renderers in the next section print respondent instructions
next to the QR code: \enquote{Choose exactly one:}, a character limit,
a scale's range.  Those words are Mentipy's, not the lecturer's, so
they come from the catalogue (\cref{chap:catalogue}) in the language
of the [[mentipy.locale]] setting (\cref{sec:locale-setting}).  A
built-in translation is not always the wording a deck wants, though.
Decks in Swedish used to rewrite the returned LaTeX with string
replacements, which broke silently whenever a release reworded a
label and needed a regular expression wherever a label carried a
number.

Each label is therefore a LaTeX macro: the slide says
[[\emph{\mentipychooseone{}}]], and the snippet defines
[[\mentipychooseone]] with [[\providecommand]], its text taken from the
catalogue.  [[\providecommand]] defines a macro only if it is not
defined yet, so a deck that defines a label before its first question
wins, and every snippet's default after that is a no-op.  The form
that works everywhere provides the label and then redefines it:
\begin{minted}{latex}
\providecommand{\mentipychooseone}{}
\renewcommand{\mentipychooseone}{Välj ett svar:}
\providecommand{\mentipycharacters}{}
\renewcommand{\mentipycharacters}{högst \mentipynumber{} tecken}
\end{minted}
Either half alone fails somewhere.  A plain [[\renewcommand]] fails
before the first snippet, because nothing has defined the label yet.  A
plain [[\newcommand]] works in the preamble, before any question, but
fails after the first question of an article document, where the
snippet's definition is global rather than local to a frame.  The pair
works in the preamble and between questions alike.

The defaults live in each snippet, as [[\mentipyqrcaption]]'s does in
each QR sidecar, rather than in the companion package
(\cref{chap:sty}).  Most decks never load that package, since
PythonTeX alone is enough to run the helpers, and a default written
into the package could not know the locale: the setting belongs to
Python, which the package reaches only once PythonTeX runs.  Inside a
Beamer frame a snippet's definition is local to the frame, so the next
frame's snippet provides it again; that costs a line and changes
nothing, because a deck's own definition is global and comes first.

A label with a number must let a translation put the number where its
language wants it: \enquote{Rank your top 3:} but \enquote{Rangordna
de 3 bästa:}.  The natural LaTeX for that, a macro with an argument
defined as [[[1]{#1 characters}]], cannot be used, because the
definition sits inside the slide's frame.  Beamer reads the body of an
ordinary frame as a macro argument before it typesets it, and a [[#]]
in that body is an illegal parameter there; a deck that pasted the
output of [[mentipy render]] into a frame would no longer compile.  The
labels therefore take no arguments at all.  The snippet sets the
number in [[\mentipynumber]], locally, just before it calls the label,
[[{\def\mentipynumber{280}\mentipycharacters{}}]], and the label's text
uses [[\mentipynumber{}]] where the catalogue has its [[{n}]] field.
The catalogue's markup walk does that substitution and escapes the
rest of the message for LaTeX.

The table names the macro of every slide message; a TeX control word
is letters only, so the names drop the underscores of the keys.

<<constants>>=
SLIDE_LABEL_MACROS = {
    "slide.choose_one": "mentipychooseone",
    "slide.choose_many": "mentipychoosemany",
    "slide.type": "mentipytypelabel",
    "slide.free_text": "mentipyfreetext",
    "slide.limit": "mentipylimitlabel",
    "slide.characters": "mentipycharacters",
    "slide.range": "mentipyrangelabel",
    "slide.low": "mentipylowlabel",
    "slide.high": "mentipyhighlabel",
    "slide.word_cloud": "mentipywordcloud",
    "slide.mode": "mentipymodelabel",
    "slide.whole_answer": "mentipywholeanswer",
    "slide.split_on": "mentipysplitlabel",
    "slide.min_word_length": "mentipyminwordlabel",
    "slide.rank_all": "mentipyrankall",
    "slide.rank_top": "mentipyranktop",
    "slide.pin_one": "mentipypinone",
    "slide.pin_many": "mentipypinmany",
    "slide.pin_comment": "mentipypincomment",
}
@

A snippet defines only the labels its body uses, so a multiple-choice
slide does not carry the word cloud's vocabulary.  The renderers
therefore ask a small collector for each label.  It returns the macro
call and remembers the key, and afterwards it writes the definitions
of the keys it was asked for, in the order they were first used.  A
label is called with an empty group, [[\mentipyfreetext{}]], which
keeps the space after it: TeX would otherwise swallow the space as the
end of the control word.  A label with a number is called in a group
that sets the number, so the number cannot leak into the next label.
Each definition line ends with [[%]], so that a snippet printed into
running text gains no stray space.

<<functions>>=
class _SlideLabels:
    def __init__(self, catalogue: Catalogue):
        self._catalogue = catalogue
        self._used: dict[str, None] = {}

    def __call__(self, key: str, number: int | None = None) -> str:
        self._used[key] = None
        call = "\\" + SLIDE_LABEL_MACROS[key] + "{}"
        if number is None:
            return call
        return f"{{\\def\\{SLIDE_NUMBER_MACRO}{{{number}}}{call}}}"

    def definitions(self) -> str:
        return "".join(
            self._definition(key) + "%\n" for key in self._used
        )

    def _definition(self, key: str) -> str:
        text = self._catalogue.markup(
            key, _escape_latex, n=f"\\{SLIDE_NUMBER_MACRO}{{}}"
        )
        return (
            f"\\providecommand{{\\{SLIDE_LABEL_MACROS[key]}}}{{{text}}}"
        )
<<constants>>=
SLIDE_NUMBER_MACRO = "mentipynumber"
@

The locale comes from the helper's [[locale=]] keyword, which a deck
sets once in its shared block (every helper takes it), and otherwise
from [[mentipy.locale]], then English.  The keyword is how a deck says
what language it is in, independent of the machine compiling it; the
setting is the lecturer's default for decks that say nothing.  The
helper records the locale its slide actually used with the question
(\cref{sec:recorded-locale}), so that the server answers in the same
language.

The catalogue is looked up when a question is rendered, that is, when
PythonTeX runs the helper.  PythonTeX reruns a block only when its code
has changed, and changing the setting changes no code, so after a
change of locale a deck must clear PythonTeX's output or build with
[[--rerun=always]], as the decks' Makefiles do; otherwise the slides
keep the labels of the earlier language.

<<functions>>=
def _slide_catalogue(locale: str | None = None) -> Catalogue:
    if locale is None:
        locale = get_setting(LOCALE_CONFIG, DEFAULT_LOCALE)
    return load_catalogue(locale)
@

Every slide message needs a macro, or the collector would fail with a
[[KeyError]] the first time a renderer asks for it, and the names must
be distinct control words.

<<test functions>>=
def test_every_slide_message_has_its_own_macro():
    slide_keys = {key for key in MESSAGES if key.startswith("slide.")}
    names = list(SLIDE_LABEL_MACROS.values())

    assert set(SLIDE_LABEL_MACROS) == slide_keys
    assert len(set(names)) == len(names)
