\section{Question body rendering}

Each question kind needs slightly different explanatory text on the left-hand
side of the slide.  Multiple-choice questions expose the largest difference:
single choice and multi-select use the same stored model, but the prompt text
must tell respondents whether one or several options are acceptable.

<<functions>>=
def _render_multiple_choice(
    question: MCQuestion, labels: _SlideLabels
) -> str:
    prompt = labels(
        "slide.choose_many" if question.multi else "slide.choose_one"
    )
    lines = ["\\emph{" + prompt + "}", "\\begin{itemize}"]
    lines.extend(
        f"\\item {_render_inline(option)}" for option in question.options
    )
    lines.append("\\end{itemize}")
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

The options go through the inline-code renderer, as the prompt does
(\cref{sec:inline-code}): backtick pairs mark code, which becomes
[[\texttt]], in the slide and the notes alike.  Escaping alone is not
enough inside it.  Typewriter type does not stop LaTeX's ligatures,
which under the T1 encoding turn [[--]] into a dash, [[@<<]] and [[>>]]
into guillemets and [[,,]] into a low quote, while [[']] becomes a curly
quote under any encoding and runs of spaces collapse.  A code piece
therefore breaks every ligature with an empty group, spells the
apostrophe as [[\textquotesingle]] and keeps each space with a control
space, so the slide shows the characters the phone shows.

<<constants>>=
LATEX_CODE_ESCAPES = {
    **LATEX_ESCAPES,
    "-": "-{}",
    "<": "<{}",
    ">": ">{}",
    ",": ",{}",
    "'": r"\textquotesingle{}",
    " ": "\\ ",
}
<<functions>>=
def _escape_latex_code(text: str) -> str:
    return "".join(LATEX_CODE_ESCAPES.get(char, char) for char in text)


def _render_inline(text: str) -> str:
    return "".join(
        f"\\texttt{{{_escape_latex_code(piece)}}}"
        if code
        else _escape_latex(piece)
        for piece, code in inline_code_spans(text)
    )
@

Open-text and scale questions are both compact enough to render as description
lists.  The labels are part of the respondent instructions, not implementation
metadata, so semantic LaTeX keeps the generated handout readable when the same
content is emitted outside a slide.

<<functions>>=
def _render_open(question: OpenQuestion, labels: _SlideLabels) -> str:
    limit = labels("slide.characters", question.max_chars)
    return "\n".join(
        [
            "\\begin{description}",
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.type')}] {labels('slide.free_text')}",
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.limit')}] {limit}",
            "\\end{description}",
        ]
    )


def _render_scale(question: ScaleQuestion, labels: _SlideLabels) -> str:
    lines = [
        "\\begin{description}",
        f"\\item[{labels('slide.range')}] {question.low}--{question.high}",
    ]

    if question.low_label:
        lines.append(
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.low')}] "
            f"{_escape_latex(question.low_label)}"
        )
    if question.high_label:
        lines.append(
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.high')}] "
            f"{_escape_latex(question.high_label)}"
        )

    lines.append("\\end{description}")
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

A word-cloud question's slide body needs to show respondents two things
the open-question body does not: that the answer will be aggregated as
a cloud, and how their answer will be cut up before it is counted.
Authors often print this on the slide so respondents understand why
their two-letter answer never appears in the cloud, or why their comma
list became three entries.  The mode in force comes from
[[effective_separators]] so the sidecar cannot drift from what the
tally does: the empty set is described as whole answers, a narrowed
set is shown verbatim (its [[repr]], so a newline is visible as
[[\n]]), and the default set says nothing beyond the minimum length
that applies to every splitting mode.

<<functions>>=
def _render_word_cloud(
    question: WordCloudQuestion, labels: _SlideLabels
) -> str:
    lines = [
        "\\begin{description}",
        f"\\item[{labels('slide.type')}] {labels('slide.word_cloud')}",
        f"\\item[{labels('slide.limit')}] "
        f"{labels('slide.characters', question.max_chars)}",
    ]
    separators = question.effective_separators()
    if separators == "":
        lines.append(
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.mode')}] "
            f"{labels('slide.whole_answer')}"
        )
    else:
        if separators is not None:
            lines.append(
                f"\\item[{labels('slide.split_on')}] "
                f"{_escape_latex(repr(separators))}"
            )
        lines.append(
            f"\\item[{labels('slide.min_word_length')}] "
            f"{question.min_word_length}"
        )
    lines.append("\\end{description}")
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

A ranking slide body lists the items to be ordered and states how many of
them the respondent should rank.  The prompt has to spell this out because
the slide is the only place a respondent learns whether a full ordering or
just a top-[[top_n]] shortlist is expected --- the form repeats the rule,
but authors typically read the prompt aloud first.  We use an enumerated
list only to give each item a stable visual handle; it does not imply a
preset order.

<<functions>>=
def _render_ranking(
    question: RankingQuestion, labels: _SlideLabels
) -> str:
    if question.top_n is None:
        prompt = labels("slide.rank_all")
    else:
        prompt = labels("slide.rank_top", question.top_n)
    lines = ["\\emph{" + prompt + "}", "\\begin{enumerate}"]
    lines.extend(
        f"\\item {_render_inline(option)}" for option in question.options
    )
    lines.append("\\end{enumerate}")
    return "\n".join(lines)
@

