\section{Web-only context}
\label{sec:context-helpers}

The context the web form shows next to a question
(\cref{sec:web-only-context}) should come from the files the slides are
built from, never be retyped: a copy drifts the moment the program on
the slides changes.  A literate deck tangles its programs into files,
so the authoring side reads those.  Two kinds of file need two helpers.
A program is \emph{shown}, by [[file_snippet]]; a data set too large
to read on a phone is \emph{offered} for download, by
[[file_download]].

What to put in a question's context deserves one rule: only the
premises.  A run of the program the question asks about, a file that
run produces, or the contrasting version of the code gives the answer
away, and so does anything the discussion after the vote will point
at, which belongs on the slide.  The helpers cannot enforce this, so
the docstrings say it.

\subsection{Showing a file}

[[file_snippet]] reads the whole file by default.  Often only part of
it is relevant: the class [[Account]] from [[bank.py]], without the
[[Bank]] that follows.  Line ranges would select that part, but they
drift whenever the file above the range changes, and a literate
program's tangled files change all the time.  A \emph{name} does not
drift, so the helper takes [[name="Account"]], or a dotted
[[name="Account.deposit"]] for a method, and finds it with [[ast]].
The file is read through [[resolve_path]] like every other file
Mentipy reads, so a deck bound into a book still finds its own files.

The language defaults to Pygments' guess from the file name, the same
lexer names the server highlights with, and to prose when Pygments has
no lexer for the file.  The label defaults to the file name, with the
definition's name after it when only part of the file is shown, so a
context of several snippets says where each one comes from.

<<functions>>=
def file_snippet(
    path: str | Path,
    *,
    name: str | None = None,
    language: str | None = None,
    label: str | None = None,
) -> ContextSnippet:
    """Return a context snippet that shows a file on the web form.

    ``name`` selects one class or function by name, dotted for a
    method (``"Account.deposit"``); by default the whole file is shown.
    ``language`` defaults to Pygments' lexer for the file name and
    ``label`` to the file name.  Show only the premises of the
    question: never a run of the program asked about, its output or
    the contrasting version.
    """

    source_path = Path(path)
    text = resolve_path(source_path).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    if name is not None:
        text = _python_definition(text, name, source_path)
    if language is None:
        language = _language_for(source_path)
    if label is None:
        label = source_path.name
        if name is not None:
            label += f": {name}"
    return ContextSnippet(text, language=language, label=label)
@

Pygments raises [[ClassNotFound]] for a file name it has no lexer for;
that is the prose case, not an error.

<<functions>>=
def _language_for(path: Path) -> str | None:
    try:
        lexer = get_lexer_for_filename(path.name)
    except ClassNotFound:
        return None
    return lexer.aliases[0] if lexer.aliases else None
@

The lookup walks the dotted name one scope at a time: the module's
top-level statements first, then the body of each class found.  The
extracted span starts at the first decorator rather than at the
[[def]] line, because [[ast]] numbers a decorated definition from its
[[def]] and dropping [[@functools.cache]] would change what the
respondent reads the code as doing.  Slicing the source lines, rather
than unparsing the tree, keeps the author's comments and layout.  A
method is dedented so it reads like the top-level code it is shown
as, by the column its definition starts in rather than by the lines'
common indentation: a docstring with lines in column 0 has no common
indentation, and would leave the whole method indented.  On a miss the
error names what the scope does contain, which is what the author
needs to fix the spelling; a definition nested in an [[if]] or a
[[try]] is not in any scope the walk visits, so the error says so
rather than listing nothing.  A name only means something in Python
source, so a file that does not parse is reported as such.

<<functions>>=
def _python_definition(source: str, name: str, path: Path) -> str:
    lines = source.splitlines(keepends=True)
    try:
        scope = ast.parse(source, filename=str(path)).body
    except SyntaxError as error:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{path}: a name needs a Python file, and this one does not"
            f" parse as Python: {error.msg}"
        ) from error
    node = None
    for part in name.split("."):
        definitions = [
            statement
            for statement in scope
            if isinstance(
                statement,
                (ast.ClassDef, ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef),
            )
        ]
        node = next(
            (
                definition
                for definition in definitions
                if definition.name == part
            ),
            None,
        )
        if node is None:
            found = ", ".join(
                definition.name for definition in definitions
            ) or "no classes or functions at that level"
            raise ValueError(
                f"{path}: no class or function {name!r}; found: {found}"
            )
        scope = node.body
    start = min(
        [node.lineno]
        + [decorator.lineno for decorator in node.decorator_list]
    )
    return "".join(
        _strip_indent(line, node.col_offset)
        for line in lines[start - 1 : node.end_lineno]
    )


def _strip_indent(line: str, indent: int) -> str:
    leading = len(line) - len(line.lstrip(" \t"))
    return line[min(indent, leading) :]
@

\subsection{Offering a file for download}

A download's bytes belong in the store's sidecar, and the store is not
known until the question helper runs, since the author passes it there
and not to [[file_download]].  The helper therefore only records the
request; [[_resolve_context]] reads the file and saves its bytes once
the store is settled.  Reading late costs nothing, because both calls
happen in the same PythonTeX block.

<<functions>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class _FileDownload:
    path: Path
    label: str | None


def file_download(
    path: str | Path, *, label: str | None = None
) -> _FileDownload:
    """Return a context item that offers a file for download.

    Use it for files too large to read on a phone, such as a data set.
    The file is read when the question helper registers the question,
    and its bytes are stored next to the store, not in it.  The
    respondent's browser saves it under its own file name.
    """

    return _FileDownload(Path(path), label)
@

\subsection{Resolving the context}

The question helpers accept a list of items, or one item on its own:
[[context=file_snippet("bank.py")]] reads more naturally than a
one-element list.  A string counts as one item, not as a sequence of
characters, and is shorthand for prose.  A [[latex]] snippet is passed
through pandoc once, with the same helper and the same fallback as a
pin-text target, so the server never needs pandoc.  An HTML rendering
already present is kept, which lets an author supply their own.

<<functions>>=
ContextItem = Union[ContextSnippet, ContextDownload, _FileDownload, str]


def _resolve_context(
    context: ContextItem | Iterable[ContextItem], store: Store
) -> tuple[ContextSnippet | ContextDownload, ...]:
    if isinstance(
        context, (str, ContextSnippet, ContextDownload, _FileDownload)
    ):
        context = (context,)
    return tuple(_resolve_context_item(item, store) for item in context)


def _resolve_context_item(
    item: ContextItem, store: Store
) -> ContextSnippet | ContextDownload:
    if isinstance(item, str):
        return ContextSnippet(item)
    if isinstance(item, _FileDownload):
        data = resolve_path(item.path).read_bytes()
        return ContextDownload(
            store.save_image(data), item.path.name, len(data), label=item.label
        )
    if isinstance(item, ContextSnippet):
        if item.language == "latex" and item.html is None:
            return replace(item, html=_latex_content_to_html(item.text))
        return item
    if isinstance(item, ContextDownload):
        return item
    raise TypeError(f"not a context item: {item!r}")
@

\section{Question snippets for Beamer frames}

The helpers return LaTeX, and the natural way to use it is
[[\printpythontex]] right after the PythonTeX block, inside the frame.
Beamer spoils that for slides with overlays: it reads a frame's body
once per overlay, so a PythonTeX block inside the frame is seen several
times, and PythonTeX's block numbering shifts between runs.  A question's
output then lands on the wrong slide.  The robust pattern keeps the
PythonTeX block outside the frame and hands the LaTeX over through a
file, which the frame [[\input]]s as often as Beamer likes:

\begin{minted}{latex}
\begin{pycode}
write_snippet("q-sequence", mc("Which runs first?", ["A", "B"]))
\end{pycode}
\begin{frame}
  \mentipyinput{q-sequence}
\end{frame}
\end{minted}

[[write_snippet]] is the writing half; the companion LaTeX package's
[[\mentipyinput]] (\cref{chap:sty}) is the reading half, which also
tolerates the first compile, before PythonTeX has written the file.  By
default the snippet goes next to the QR codes, in the configured QR
directory, because that directory already holds generated files the
author does not edit.  The file is resolved against the base directory
like any other, and the returned path is the LaTeX-facing one.

We skip the write when the content is unchanged.  The file's timestamp
then stays put, so build tools that watch dependencies do not see a
change on every run.

<<functions>>=
def write_snippet(
    name: str,
    latex: str,
    *,
    directory: str | Path | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Write `latex` to `<directory>/<name>.tex` and return that path.

    `directory` defaults to the configured QR directory.  The file is
    written relative to the base directory, if one is set, while the
    returned path is the one to use from LaTeX.  Use it to keep
    PythonTeX blocks out of Beamer frames: write the question's LaTeX
    here and `\\input` the file inside the frame.
    """

    chosen_dir = Path(directory or get_setting(QR_DIR_CONFIG, DEFAULT_QR_DIR))
    snippet = chosen_dir / f"{name}.tex"
    target = resolve_path(snippet)
    if not target.exists() or target.read_text(encoding="utf-8") != latex:
        target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        target.write_text(latex, encoding="utf-8")
    return snippet.as_posix()
@

\section{Rendering contracts}

The default tests pin the existing slide contract: each helper must register the
question, write the QR code to the configured output directory, and mention
that file in the returned LaTeX.  The tests reference [[DEFAULT_QR_DIR]] from
the store rather than the literal directory name, so renaming the default in
one place reaches every assertion automatically.

